---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CrossLinks from '../components/CrossLinks.astro';
import Receipt from '../components/Receipt.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="How I replaced an agency handoff process with an AI-native design-to-code pipeline"
  metaTitle="An AI-native design-to-code pipeline | Tyler Young"
  eyebrow="AI-Native Practice"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    [
      'Contribution',
      'Built and ran a Figma↔Claude Code pipeline producing production React, Tailwind and Storybook components',
    ],
    ['Role', 'Director of Design · 90-day engagement · 2026'],
    ['Scope', 'Design system v0, pipeline design, stakeholder process change'],
    ['Takeaway', '[NEEDS: Tyler’s one-liner]'],
  ]}
>
  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Open decision — client naming. This page currently avoids naming the company.
      Name it, or describe it as “an AR / asset-management company”? Tyler’s call.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Challenge</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      An aged brand and an aging design system, with an agency sitting between design
      intent and shipped UI. Every decision made a round trip through a handoff
      document. The mandate was to modernize the look and feel and to design the
      applications that came next.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The problem was not the agency's craft. It was the distance the work had to
      travel before anyone could see whether it was right.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>My Response</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A 90-day plan built around one change: replace the handoff with a pipeline.
      Figma to Claude Code to React, Tailwind and Storybook, with the design system
      as the thing that constrains what gets generated.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Design system v0 came out of real surfaces — the landing page, the context
      engine, the workflow designer — not from a component wishlist. Stakeholder
      reviews moved onto working code.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Receipt
    label="~/design-system — prompt to rendered component"
    leftLabel="Instruction"
    rightLabel="Rendered in Storybook"
    caption="The loop that replaced the handoff document. Capture pending."
  >
    <div slot="left">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">
        3–4 stills or a 60–90s capture: prompt in Claude Code → generated component
        → rendered result in Storybook
      </Needs>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">Storybook screenshot — component grid / library view</Needs>
    </div>
  </Receipt>

  <Receipt
    label="handoff.pdf vs. main@HEAD"
    leftLabel="Agency-era artifact"
    rightLabel="Pipeline output"
    caption="The before/after that makes the argument without a paragraph of process description."
  >
    <div slot="left">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">One agency-era handoff artifact, redacted as needed</Needs>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">The equivalent surface, produced through the pipeline</Needs>
    </div>
  </Receipt>

  <h2>The Win</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      what actually changed. Honest scale: 90 days, v0, process proved — “stakeholders
      reviewed working code for the first time” is strong enough without embellishment
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <h2>Key Outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>3–5 real, verifiable statements — no invented metrics</Needs>
  </p>

  <h2>Strategic Insight</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The pipeline is the design system's delivery mechanism, not a tool bolted beside
      it. Reviews on working code collapse the gap between what was drawn and what was
      understood.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      When exploration costs almost nothing, the constraint moves. It stops being how
      many options a team can produce and becomes which ones are worth building, and
      what guardrails they have to respect on the way out.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Held back pending Tyler's decision: the team-assessment thread (baseline,
      retain / retrain / replace recommendation). Public, or interview-only?
    </p>
  </div>

  <CrossLinks
    slot="after"
    label="Related"
    title="The operating model behind this"
    links={[
      { href: '/how-i-design-with-ai', label: 'How I design with AI' },
      { href: '/word-web', label: 'Word web — the same system shipping a product' },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>
